---
/**
 * foodcraft / CredentialsBN — "07 / Standards" (Panday Coffee Works).
 *
 * ROLE: the design's CREDENTIALS element — the shop's standing on record, set
 * as a RULED SPECIFICATION TABLE rather than a row of badges. One headline cell
 * across the top of the content column, then a flush four-up grid of cells
 * drawn entirely in hairlines: a rust mono numeral, a 10.5px tracked-caps mono
 * title, a 13.5px sentence. No card, no shadow, no radius, no seal, no photo.
 * It is the one band on this page whose whole job is a claim, which is exactly
 * why not one word of it is written here.
 *
 * DESIGN FIDELITY — every value below is lifted from the element carrying
 * data-component="CREDENTIALS" in "Panday Coffee Works Landing.dc.html", with
 * each hex mapped to the token holding that same value (R1). No literal hex
 * appears in this file:
 *   section     grid minmax(0,52px) minmax(0,1fr), bottom rule 1px #17181A
 *               .......... the wrapper's .sec, --rail-w and --rule-strong
 *   rail        right hairline #C8C7C2, content bottom-aligned, label rotated
 *               mono 10px/.3em #A8351B ..... the wrapper's .rail and .rail-t
 *   head cell   padding 44px 34px 26px ..... .sec-body already tops out at
 *               exactly 44px/34px (clamp(28,4vw,44) · clamp(20,3vw,34)); only
 *               the 26px BOTTOM is local, because .sec-body's own bottom
 *               clamps to 40px for the sections that end in body copy
 *   headline    clamp(28px,3.6vw,50px) / line-height 1 / -.03em / uppercase /
 *               margin 0 / #17181A ......... .h2 for face, weight, tracking,
 *               transform and var(--ink); size, leading, margin and the measure
 *               cap are restated — DECISION 2
 *   cells grid  repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr)), border-top 1px #C8C7C2 ... var(--line)
 *   cell        padding 26px 22px, border-right AND border-bottom 1px #C8C7C2
 *               .......................................... var(--line)
 *   numeral     IBM Plex Mono 11px #A8351B, margin-bottom 12px, no weight and
 *               no line-height declared in the design ..... the wrapper's .fig
 *               (mono, tabular lining figures, var(--accent)) plus a local
 *               400 / 11px / normal / display:block — DECISION 4
 *   title       IBM Plex Mono 10.5px / .14em / 600 / uppercase / #17181A,
 *               margin 0 0 9px, no line-height declared .... var(--mono) over
 *               the wrapper's h1–h4 reset, which already supplies var(--ink)
 *               and the uppercase
 *   body        13.5px / 1.72 / #3C3E42 / margin 0 ......... var(--ink-2)
 * #3C3E42 is --ink-2 exactly, not --ink-soft (#5A5C60): this design uses the
 * darker grey for reading copy and reserves --ink-soft/--muted for mono labels.
 * Every colour this element uses has a token; nothing had to be invented.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — all four are declared in components/editor/
 * genericContentSchema.ts under section id 'credentials', so none is a dead
 * hook:
 *   credentials.tag ................ the eyebrow — DECISION 1
 *   credentials.headline ........... the h2, fed with set:html so an owner can
 *                                    place this design's rust second voice
 *                                    inside their OWN sentence
 *   credentials.items.${i}.title ... the tracked-caps cell title
 *   credentials.items.${i}.body .... the sentence under it. The schema declares
 *                                    {title, body} and the emitted hook always
 *                                    spells `body`; the reader below also
 *                                    ACCEPTS label / desc / detail / description
 *                                    as input, because lib/services/groq.service
 *                                    writes {label, detail} and normalizeBlock
 *                                    copies body into desc.
 *
 * DERIVED, and therefore carrying NO data-field and no stored value:
 *   · the rail label "07 / Standards". It is the design's own literal for this
 *     element — the eighth RAILED label in the sheet (HERO 00, ABOUT 01,
 *     SERVICES 02, WHY 03, GALLERY 04, HOW 05, TESTIMONIALS 06, CREDENTIALS 07,
 *     LOCATION 08, SERVICE-AREA 09, FAQ 10, FOOTER 11). It is NOT the section's
 *     ordinal in PageBN's render order, and the difference is not a rounding
 *     error: the header and TRUST carry no rail in the design and take no
 *     index, so Credentials is the tenth thing PageBN renders and the eighth
 *     thing the spine counts. The label is decorative marginalia, never an
 *     editable hook, and it must be renumbered by hand if the wrapper ever
 *     reorders its sections or gives TRUST a rail.
 *   · the cell numeral (01, 02, 03 …) is built from the DISPLAY index, so the
 *     list always counts without gaps however many blank rows were dropped and
 *     an edit can never be misdirected into a counter (R6). The design's own
 *     seed data is zero-padded to two digits ('01'…'04'); padStart reproduces
 *     that. aria-hidden, since the title beside it is the content (R9).
 *   · the track count: four is the design, but a shorter list takes exactly as
 *     many tracks as it has rows, so three credentials fill the measure instead
 *     of leaving a ruled empty quarter at the right-hand end.
 *   · the grid's top hairline, which is dropped when there is no head. With a
 *     head it rules between the headline and the cells exactly as drawn; with
 *     no head it would land 0px under the previous section's full-strength ink
 *     rule and print as a doubled line.
 *
 * R2 — NOTHING IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE. content.credentials has no derived
 * fallback anywhere: lib/block-defaults.ts ships `credentials: []`,
 * derive-content-defaults never invents one, and astro-builder passes
 * `c.credentials ?? undefined` into keepHead. Consequences, all deliberate:
 *   · with no filled row the WHOLE <section> is omitted — no rail, no rules, no
 *     headline, no padding. The gate counts the ROWS and never the tag or the
 *     headline (R5), because keepHead can backfill those two from a derived
 *     block and a gate reading them would ship a heading over nothing.
 *   · title and body are guarded independently and OUTSIDE their tags, so a
 *     half-filled row prints only what was typed — never an empty <h3> holding
 *     9px of margin open under the numeral, and never a rule under a row that
 *     is not there.
 *   · those guards are trim-AWARE only because the reader coerces and trims
 *     once, before anything else touches the value: a title of "   " is truthy,
 *     and guarded raw it would ride through the row filter on its sibling's
 *     body and print exactly that empty heading.
 *   · the coercion also matters for a NUMBER: an owner (or an older AI save)
 *     who put `2019` in a credential title has no .trim() to call, and calling
 *     it in the row filter would throw and take the whole build down.
 *   · the design's demo strings ("Direct trade", "Roast date stamped", "SCA
 *     trained", "City permitted") are the DESIGNER filling in their own
 *     template. A shop that never sent anyone to SCA training must not have
 *     this page say it did, and an unpermitted kitchen must not be captioned as
 *     inspected. Those four sentences are the single most dangerous paragraph
 *     of demo copy on the page, and none of it is reproduced here.
 *   · NO seal, shield, ribbon, tick, stamp or logo strip. A drawn badge is a
 *     visual claim of certification the owner never made. The design draws none
 *     either: the numeral and the hairline are the whole ornament.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED, so none of it reads as an oversight:
 *   · NO star row. R2's rating rule is real but has nothing to bind to here — a
 *     credential carries no numeric rating field in the schema, and five drawn
 *     stars for a shop that gave no rating would be a fabricated claim. This
 *     design draws its stars nowhere at all; TESTIMONIALS is the band that
 *     carries reviews.
 *   · NO onPage() helper, no PAGE_ANCHORS, no id. This section emits NO href of
 *     any kind — the design gives CREDENTIALS no link and no button — so the
 *     helper would be an unused const with nothing to remap, and 'credentials'
 *     is not one of PageBN's ids (menu about gallery visit faq main) so nothing
 *     links here either. R7 exists to stop an anchor pointing at an id this page
 *     has not got, not to require a helper where there is no anchor.
 *     CredentialsBL and CredentialsBM record the same decision. If a later
 *     revision adds an action to this band, copy the helper from HeaderBN /
 *     ServicesBN wholesale rather than hand-rolling a shorter one, with
 *     PAGE_ANCHORS = menu about gallery visit faq main and the family's
 *     ANCHOR_ALIAS (location|contact→visit, services|rooms→menu, host|why→
 *     about, work→gallery, book→main).
 *   · NO button, so none of the forbidden theme-engine class names
 *     (.btn-primary .btn-ghost .nav-cta .cta-band .trust .testi) appears here,
 *     and `.hero` is not on this <section> (R4). Where a button IS wanted in
 *     this family the wrapper's own four are the only ones used: .btn--fill,
 *     .btn--ink, .btn--orange, .btn--cream.
 *   · NO dark ground. The design keeps CREDENTIALS on --paper; HOW-IT-WORKS and
 *     the closing CTA band are the only two grounds that leave it.
 *   · NO .plate / .ph and no role="img". The design gives this band no
 *     photograph, so there is no upload to offer and no accessible name to
 *     invent for one (R9).
 *   · NO data-block marker: editorBridge only needs one where two hook prefixes
 *     share a <section>, and everything here is credentials.*.
 *
 * DECISIONS A READER MIGHT OTHERWISE QUESTION:
 *   1. THE EYEBROW IS BOUND EVEN THOUGH THE DESIGN DRAWS NONE HERE. The design
 *      uses the .24em mono kicker exactly twice in the whole document (the hero
 *      and the CTA band) and CREDENTIALS is not one of them — but
 *      genericContentSchema declares credentials.tag and the sidebar prints an
 *      "Eyebrow tag" box for it, so leaving it unbound would swallow a string
 *      an owner typed with no way to tell they had. It costs nothing in
 *      fidelity: groq.service emits credentials as a BARE ARRAY with no tag,
 *      keepHead's fallback for this block is undefined, and block-defaults
 *      ships [], so the eyebrow is absent on every generated site and appears
 *      only where a human typed one — the default rendering is the design
 *      exactly as drawn. The gap it opens above the headline is the design's
 *      own kicker→headline gap, .14em of the headline's size, taken from the
 *      hero (the only place the design pairs the two) and applied only when the
 *      eyebrow is actually there.
 *   2. THE HEADLINE SCALE IS RESTATED, AND THE OVERRIDE IS WRITTEN AS A DOUBLED
 *      CLASS SO IT KEEPS WINNING. This design's h2 is
 *      clamp(28px,3.6vw,50px) at line-height 1 — the same in all seven of its
 *      headed sections (ABOUT, SERVICES, GALLERY, HOW, TESTIMONIALS,
 *      CREDENTIALS, FAQ) — where the wrapper's shared .h2 is
 *      clamp(28px,4.4vw,62px) at .98 with a 20ch cap.
 *      THE CASCADE ARITHMETIC, re-derived from the compiled CSS rather than
 *      from memory, because this block twice stated it wrong: astro.config.mjs
 *      sets no scopedStyleStrategy, so Astro's DEFAULT applies and that default
 *      is "attribute" — a scoped `.pdcr-h` compiles to
 *      `.pdcr-h[data-astro-cid-…]`, one class plus one attribute, (0,2,0). The
 *      wrapper's `html[data-page="BN"] :where(.h2)` is (0,1,1), where the ONE
 *      class-column term is the `[data-page="BN"]` ATTRIBUTE (`:where(.h2)`
 *      contributes ZERO) and `html` is the type. (0,2,0) > (0,1,1), so a bare
 *      local class would already win, at every width and inside the 760 block
 *      too; `.pdcr-h.h2` is (0,3,0) and wins by more. The doubling is therefore
 *      not what makes these declarations land TODAY — it is the margin that
 *      keeps them landing if scopedStyleStrategy is ever set to 'where', which
 *      collapses every single-class rule here to (0,1,0) and hands the wrapper
 *      the win. Same for `.pdcr-head.sec-body` below; `.pdcr-n.fig` is (0,3,0)
 *      and `.pdcr-cell .pdcr-t` / `.pdcr-cell .pdcr-p` are (0,4,0), all of them
 *      clear of the wrapper either way.
 *      Winning inside the wrapper's `@media (max-width:760px)` block is
 *      required rather than incidental: that pass resolves to 40px at 759px
 *      while this band's own clamp is already floored at 28px by 778px, so
 *      letting the shared rule win would make the headline JUMP FROM 28px TO
 *      40px as the viewport NARROWS past 760. The design's clamp is monotonic
 *      at every breakpoint here — 50px ≥1389 · 36.9px @1024 · 32.4px @900 ·
 *      28px @778 · 28px @520 · 28px @360 — and is therefore never restated in a
 *      media query below.
 *   3. THE MEASURE CAP IS RELEASED, WHICH THE OTHER RESTATEMENTS ARE NOT. The
 *      wrapper puts max-width:20ch on .h2; no h2 in this design carries a
 *      max-width, and this one sits in a head cell that spans the whole content
 *      column — at a 1440px viewport that cell is 1440 − 52 (rail) − 68 (the
 *      .sec-body sides) = 1320px wide. `ch` is the advance of the "0" glyph, so
 *      at the 50px cap 20ch of Archivo is on the order of 550-600px: the cap
 *      would hold the headline to about twenty characters a line in a cell more
 *      than twice that wide. Released rather than raised, because a
 *      chosen-by-me ch value would be an invented number where the design
 *      states one: none.
 *   4. THE NUMERAL'S WEIGHT AND LEADING ARE RESTATED TO THE DESIGN'S OWN
 *      VALUES, not to chosen ones. The design's numeral div declares neither,
 *      so it inherits weight 400 from body (which sets none either) and
 *      computes line-height to `normal`; the wrapper's .fig ships 500 and
 *      line-height 1, which would print an 11px line box where the design draws
 *      ~14px (IBM Plex Mono's normal leading is ~1.3em). Half-leading splits
 *      evenly, so roughly 1.6px of that comes off UNDER the glyph and tightens
 *      the 12px the design sets below the numeral — the margin itself is
 *      unaffected. Everything else — the mono face, var(--accent),
 *      tabular lining figures — arrives from .fig untouched, and the tabular
 *      figures are the one thing .fig adds that the design does not state (they
 *      cannot change the drawing: 01–04 are the same width either way).
 *   5. THE RIGHT-EDGE HAIRLINE IS KEPT IN EVERY LAYOUT STATE. The design puts
 *      border-right on every repeated cell including the fourth, and the
 *      content column runs to the viewport edge (--maxw is 100%), so that last
 *      hairline IS the sheet's right-hand rule. It is kept when the grid
 *      collapses to two tracks and to one, so the page keeps being ruled on
 *      both sides all the way down.
 *   6. A TRAILING HALF-ROW IS LEFT AS THE DESIGN DRAWS IT. The bottom hairline
 *      is per-cell, verbatim from the design's sc-for, so five or six
 *      credentials end with a rule that runs part of the measure. That is the
 *      drawing the design specifies, and it reads as a ruled list that stops
 *      rather than a grid with a hole in it. The section's own full-strength
 *      ink rule closes it underneath regardless.
 *   7. THE DIVIDERS ARE BORDERS, never box-shadows. Grid items paint in
 *      order-modified document order, each atomically (CSS Grid §6.2), so a
 *      hairline cast as a shadow from one cell lands under its neighbour's own
 *      box and renders nowhere.
 *   8. THE RAIL IS aria-hidden. Its label is a derived index written by this
 *      file, not owner copy, and it announces nothing the headline and the
 *      cells do not. ONE literal aria-hidden on the rail div and none on the
 *      span inside it — never a literal beside a conditional one (R9).
 *
 * R3 — one scoped <style> block, no is:global. The headline is injected with
 * set:html, so scoped rules cannot reach an <em> an owner put inside it; its
 * rust second voice comes from PageBN's global `:where(.h2 em)` (which also
 * pins font-style:normal, because the emphasis in this design is a COLOUR
 * CHANGE and Archivo has no italic worth using), so no :global() rule is needed
 * below. No literal end-style-tag is written inside any comment here or in the
 * stylesheet — the parser would end the sheet there and truncate the file.
 *
 * CONTRAST — computed for this file from the wrapper's palette (WCAG 2.x,
 * linearised sRGB, (L1+.05)/(L2+.05)), not carried over from anywhere:
 *   --ink   #17181A on --paper #EFEEEA = 15.30:1 (the 10.5px titles)
 *   --ink-2 #3C3E42 on --paper          =  9.23:1 (the 13.5px sentences)
 *   --accent #A8351B on --paper         =  5.67:1 (the numerals and the rail
 *                                         label — both decorative and
 *                                         aria-hidden, and AA either way)
 * The eyebrow is the same 5.67:1 accent at 10px, which clears AA for normal
 * text. All three agree with PageBN's own table, which records ink/paper 15.30
 * and accent/paper 5.67 for the same hexes. (An earlier draft of this block
 * claimed the wrapper recorded accent/paper as 6.71, and defended the number as
 * the ratio against #FFFFFF. The wrapper records 5.67, and white is not in this
 * palette at all — nothing in this design ever paints --accent on it, so no
 * ratio against it is quoted here.)
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBN hands it to all fifteen sections
// uniformly. This band reads nothing outside content.*, so it is never
// destructured.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any;
}
const { content } = Astro.props;


// The schema declares 'credentials.items' with fallbackPaths ['credentials'],
// so the stored value is legitimately EITHER {tag, headline, items:[…]} or a
// bare array — groq.service writes the bare array. Both shapes are read.
const cred = content.credentials || {};

// COERCE, THEN TRIM, ONCE AT THE READER. See R2 in the block above for the two
// reasons (a number has no .trim(); every guard downstream must be trim-aware).
// Trimming is not supplying content: the owner's words are untouched, only the
// whitespace around them.
const str = (v: unknown): string =>
  typeof v === 'string' ? v : typeof v === 'number' && Number.isFinite(v) ? String(v) : '';

// The eyebrow and the headline are credentials.* ONLY. The design's own
// "How we keep it honest" is the designer filling in their template; it is not
// this file's to type.
const tag = str(cred.tag).trim();
const headline = str(cred.headline).trim();

type Item = {
  title?: unknown; label?: unknown;
  body?: unknown; desc?: unknown; detail?: unknown; description?: unknown;
};
const items: Item[] = Array.isArray(cred.items)
  ? cred.items
  : (Array.isArray(cred) ? cred : []);

// `label` is groq.service's key for the same field the schema calls `title`.
const titleOf = (it: Item): string => (str(it.title).trim() || str(it.label).trim());
// Reads the aliases as INPUT (older saves, groq's `detail`, and normalizeBlock's
// desc copy) but the data-field emitted below always spells the canonical
// `body`. `||` rather than `??` deliberately: normalizeBlock copies body into
// desc whenever desc is merely absent, so a row saved with body:'' and a real
// desc must fall through rather than stop on the empty string.
const bodyOf = (it: Item): string =>
  (str(it.body).trim() || str(it.desc).trim() || str(it.detail).trim() || str(it.description).trim());

// R6 — a row an owner added but never filled would otherwise draw a numeral,
// two hairlines and 52px of padding over nothing. Drop the blanks (whitespace
// counts as blank) but carry the ORIGINAL index, so every data-field still
// writes to the real array slot: removing credential 2 must never redirect an
// edit into credential 3. The numeral uses the DISPLAY index instead, because
// it is decorative and must keep counting 01, 02, 03 without gaps.
const rows = items
  .map((it, i: number) => ({ ...it, i }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(titleOf(r) || bodyOf(r)));

const hasHead = Boolean(tag || headline);
// Four tracks is the design; a shorter list takes exactly its own row count so
// it fills the measure instead of ruling an empty quarter at the right end.
const cellCols = Math.min(rows.length, 4);
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="sec pdcr">
    {/* Derived marginalia, not content — hidden from the tree once, here, and
        never again on the span inside (R9). */}
    <div class="rail" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rail-t">Standards</span></div>
    <div class="pdcr-col">
      {hasHead && (
        <div class="sec-body pdcr-head reveal">
          {tag && <span class="kick pdcr-kick" data-field="credentials.tag">{tag}</span>}
          {headline && (
            <h2 class="h2 pdcr-h" data-field="credentials.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>
          )}
        </div>
      )}
      <div
        class:list={['pdcr-grid', 'reveal', `pdcr-grid--${cellCols}`, !hasHead && 'pdcr-grid--flush']}
        data-delay="1"
      >
        {rows.map((it, k: number) => (
          <div class="pdcr-cell">
            <span class="fig pdcr-n" aria-hidden="true">{String(k + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            {titleOf(it) && (
              <h3 class="pdcr-t" data-field={`credentials.items.${it.i}.title`}>{titleOf(it)}</h3>
            )}
            {bodyOf(it) && (
              <p class="pdcr-p" data-field={`credentials.items.${it.i}.body`}>{bodyOf(it)}</p>
            )}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}

<style>
  /* The section itself is the wrapper's .sec — the rail grid and the
     full-strength bottom rule both arrive from the spine and are not restated,
     so this band cannot drift from its fourteen siblings. */
  .pdcr { min-width: 0; }

  /* The content column. The design wraps the head and the grid in ONE unpadded
     div: the head carries its own padding and the grid runs flush to both
     edges, which is why .sec-body is on the head rather than on this column. */
  .pdcr-col { min-width: 0; }

  /* .sec-body supplies the design's 44px top and 34px sides at its cap. Only
     the bottom is local: the design closes this head at 26px, where .sec-body
     clamps to 40px for the sections that end in body copy. Traced across the
     ladder — 26px ≥1182 · 19.8px @900 · 18px ≤818 — it only ever shrinks as the
     viewport narrows.
     WRITTEN `.pdcr-head.sec-body` — see DECISION 2. Under the project's default
     scoping ('attribute') a bare `.pdcr-head` compiles to
     `.pdcr-head[data-astro-cid-…]` at (0,2,0) and already clears the wrapper's
     `html[data-page="BN"] :where(.sec-body)` at (0,1,1); doubled it is (0,3,0),
     which is what still clears it if the strategy is ever switched to 'where'
     and the bare class drops to (0,1,0). */
  .pdcr-head.sec-body { padding-bottom: clamp(18px, 2.2vw, 26px); }

  /* The eyebrow IS the wrapper's .kick, so this band cannot fork from the two
     places the design draws one and cannot lose the family's voice. The only
     thing ADDED is a wrap rule the wrapper never sets: .kick is an inline-block
     carrying .24em of tracking, so one long unbroken owner word is unusually
     wide for its character count and would push the document sideways at 360px
     (R8). */
  .pdcr-kick { max-width: 100%; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* The design's headline, restated: see DECISION 2 for why the size and
     leading must keep winning inside the wrapper's 760 pass, and DECISION 3 for
     why the 20ch cap is released rather than raised. Face, weight, tracking,
     transform, colour and the rust <em> all still come from the shared .h2 and
     its global em rule.
     WRITTEN `.pdcr-h.h2` — the doubled class is the cascade margin described in
     DECISION 2, not a requirement of today's default scoping; all five
     declarations below land either way. */
  .pdcr-h.h2 {
    font-size: clamp(28px, 3.6vw, 50px);
    line-height: 1;
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* The design pairs a kicker with a headline in exactly one place — the hero,
     whose h1 opens the gap with margin-top .14em. Borrowed verbatim, and only
     when the eyebrow is actually rendered, so the headline keeps the design's
     margin:0 on every site that has no tag. Doubled and placed AFTER the rule
     above so it wins margin-top on source order as well as on specificity. */
  .pdcr-kick + .pdcr-h.h2 { margin-top: .14em; }

  /* THE GRID. Flush to both edges of the content column, drawn entirely with
     the cells' own borders plus one hairline across the top. Track count is
     derived — see the frontmatter — so a short list never rules an empty
     column. */
  .pdcr-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .pdcr-grid--1 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .pdcr-grid--2 { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .pdcr-grid--3 { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .pdcr-grid--4 { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  /* With no head above it, the top hairline would sit 0px under the previous
     section's full-strength ink rule and print as a doubled line. */
  .pdcr-grid--flush { border-top: 0; }

  /* The design's cell, verbatim. Both borders are kept in every layout state —
     DECISION 5 for the right-hand one, DECISION 6 for the trailing row. */
  .pdcr-cell {
    padding: 26px 22px;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* The rust numeral. .fig already carries the mono face, var(--accent) and
     tabular lining figures; the block display, the size, the weight and the
     leading are local, all four to the design's own values — DECISION 4.
     Written `.pdcr-n.fig` so it clears the wrapper's :where(.fig) whatever the
     source order. */
  .pdcr-n.fig {
    display: block;
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: normal;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  /* PageBN's h1–h4 reset is Archivo 800 at -.03em and line-height .98, so the
     face, weight, size, tracking, leading and margin are all restated to reach
     the design's mono tracked caps. Colour and the uppercase come from that
     reset and are deliberately NOT repeated. The design declares no
     line-height here, so `normal` is its value rather than a chosen one.
     Qualified with the cell class to clear the (0,1,1) reset with room to
     spare. */
  .pdcr-cell .pdcr-t {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 10.5px;
    line-height: normal;
    letter-spacing: .14em;
    margin: 0 0 9px;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* PageBN's `:where(p){margin:0 0 1em}` is (0,1,1); qualifying with the cell
     class puts this clear of it so the design's zero margin actually lands.
     #3C3E42 is --ink-2, not --ink-soft. */
  .pdcr-cell .pdcr-p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.72;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── 1024 · R8. The grid runs flush to the content column, so at a 1024px
        viewport it is 1024 − 52 (the rail) = 972px, four tracks of 243px. Take
        off the cell's 44px of horizontal padding and 199px of it is text —
        roughly thirty characters a line at 13.5px, taking an average advance of
        about half the font size — well under a readable measure. Two tracks,
        which is still the design's flush ruled grid, just wider. */
  @media (max-width: 1024px) {
    .pdcr-grid--3,
    .pdcr-grid--4 { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  }

  /* ── 900 · R8, the full collapse. Every track count is one column here, so no
        multi-column grid survives past 900px. The paragraph picks up the
        design's own 66ch measure (the value it sets on the WHY-US body, the
        only place it caps one) because a single cell now spans the whole sheet:
        at 900px that is 900 − 52 − 44 = 804px of text, which at 13.5px is
        comfortably past a hundred characters a line on the same half-the-font-
        size estimate used above.
        The headline is NOT re-clamped: its own clamp is already at its 28px
        floor by 778px, and restating a larger floor in a narrower query is
        exactly what makes type grow as a viewport shrinks (DECISION 2). */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .pdcr-grid--2,
    .pdcr-grid--3,
    .pdcr-grid--4 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .pdcr-cell .pdcr-p { max-width: 66ch; }
  }

  /* No 520 block. The wrapper owns the shared 520 pass (it drops .kick to
     9.5px), and nothing local needs to move: the cell's 22px of horizontal
     padding still leaves 316px of column at 360px, every string in the band
     breaks anywhere, and there is no button here to hold a label. */
</style>
